## mutation b (strict variant `=== 0`: diverging triples scheduled, only collinear skipped)
  fortune.ts:338; observed: 0 tests ran. Describe-time `run(sites)` in the suite throws
  "internal error: edge 0,2 has 1 vertices and 2 infinite ends" (assemble, fortune.ts:514), so the whole suite fails (26 of 26).
  The b2 entry above (`> 0`, collinear triple reaches circumcentre) shows the per-test spread for a milder variant.

Notes
- (a) is caught by exactly one test, the trace check "lets D split the arc of A and cancel that event". Without the cancel at
  the split, the stale event stays pending and is cancelled by a later cancelBroken as 'neighbour'; no random test saw it
  popped stale, so the effect is the trace position and the reason ('split' vs 'neighbour'), not the diagram.
- (c) is caught only by the new test "pops circle events of equal exact height by increasing x, which doubles can invert"
  (see 22-doubles.log: doubles ties are broken wrongly only for exact rational heights shared by distinct circles).
- (d) also fails the new direct hit test "returns 0 exactly at the breakpoint of arcs with unequal heights".
- (e) is the tie-convention flip (circle before site at an equal height and x); only the new test "pops a circle event
  before a site at its lowest point" fails, which fits the finding that the diagram is unchanged by the flip.
